  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 216 PM CST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\nThis afternoon, a closed low was analyzed over the southern Canadian \nPrairies, with cyclonic flow across the Northern Plains. Mostly\nclear skies were prevailing across the the state in the wake of\na weak surface trough and precipitation that moved through \novernight. The aforementioned surface low to our north has \ndeepened and led to a tight surface pressure gradient, with \nbreezy to windy conditions across the area this afternoon. \nSustained winds are around 25 mph in the southwest, with \noccasional gusts in the upper 30 mph range. Highs this afternoon\nwill be in the upper 40s to mid 50s, outperforming the \ndeterministic NBM and 75th percentile by a few degrees. Quiet \nbut a bit breezy still tonight, with lows in the upper 20s to \nmid 30s. \n\nAs the upper low continues to slowly push off to the east, a \ncold front is progged to drop south through the day Saturday, \nbringing breezy to strong winds, precipitation chances, and \ncooler, cloudy conditions. \n\nThe strongest surface pressure rises in deterministic guidance are \nwell to our southeast, and look relatively marginal across the \nforecast area. This, combined with the ECMWF EFI consistently \nadvertising relatively low values, has us favoring holding off \non a Wind Advisory for Saturday at this time. Can't rule out \nneeding one for a county or two in southwest North Dakota, but \nwill see how things trend through tonight. \n\nThere's also relatively high confidence in precipitation \noccurring with this front, although forecast soundings show a \nmodest dry level right near the surface, so we could see some \nvirga as well. Opted to broad-brush low PoPs through Saturday \nand Saturday night, as high- res guidance has been consistent \nin advertising showery precipitation, just low confidence in \nwhere and when. This could be rain, snow, or a rain-snow mix, \ndepending on timing and location, as cooler air filters in \nbehind the front with lows Saturday night in the 20s. \n\nNorthwesterly flow aloft and tomorrow's front will usher in\ncooler air for the start of next week, with highs mainly in the\n30s Sunday and Monday. Ensemble guidance favors broad split flow\npotentially developing for next week, with NBM temperature\npercentiles advertising above normal temperatures and very low\nprecipitation chances through the beginning of December.",
